## prd.md

Follow `.ai/rules/prd.md` and the playbook `.ai/skills/write-prd/SKILL.md`. `.ai/specs/FEAT-000-hello-world/` is a worked example of the exact format.

- First lines: `# {{feature}}: <short title>`, then `Status: Draft (generated from {{sourceName}}; a human must review it before implementation)`.
  The title names the **feature**, in two to four words (`Customer Login`, `Password Reset`), not the document (`Requirements`): the spec folder is named after it.
- **Objective**, then **User stories & acceptance criteria**, then Permissions & roles, Data & validation, Contract impact, <%#designEnforced%>**UI & design** (see below), <%/designEnforced%>Out of scope, Verification map.
- Every acceptance criterion is one line: `- **AC-001** Given ..., when ..., then ... (Source: <page or section of the document>)`.
  - One observable behaviour per criterion. Observable from outside: HTTP status and body, page content, a command's result, a file.
  - Include the unhappy paths the document implies: validation errors, not found, unauthorized, boundaries (0, 1, max, max+1), empty states.
  - Quote numbers, limits, status codes and wording **exactly as the document states them**.
  - **Never invent requirements.** If a criterion cannot be made testable without filling a gap, use the repository's conventions
    (<%#hasBackend%>`.ai/rules/contract.md`<%/hasBackend%><%^hasBackend%>`<%clientRuleFile%>`<%/hasBackend%>) and record the gap in the last section as a line starting with `ASSUMPTION:`.
  - Requirements that cannot be verified through HTTP, commands or files (<%^designEnforced%>visual design, <%/designEnforced%>performance targets, process, policy) do **not** become
    criteria: list them under "Out of scope / not machine-verifiable" with their source.<%#designEnforced%>
  - What a screen looks like (layout, states, copy, the frame in the design file) goes in the **UI & design** section, not in criteria, and is never invented: see below.<%/designEnforced%>
- Finish with a section **Assumptions & open questions**: every assumption, ambiguity or question for the document's author, one per line. Nothing is silently decided.
- No `TODO(prd)` markers anywhere.

<%#designEnforced%>
## UI & design (a section of prd.md, always present)

Heading `## 6. UI & design`. If the document says nothing about screens for this scope, the section is the single line `No UI change.` Otherwise it lists, from the document only:
the design source (a link or frame name the document gives, else `none`), each screen and its states (loading, empty, error, success), the components it reuses, the new
design tokens it needs (colours, spacing, sizes the document names; else `none`), the exact copy, and responsive and accessibility behaviour. Anything the document leaves open becomes an
`ASSUMPTION:` line. Do not write `.ai/design/tokens.json`: that file is not yours to change.

<%/designEnforced%>
## acceptance.json

Format: `{ "feature": "{{feature}}", "checks": [ ... ] }`. Every acceptance criterion needs at least one check; every check lists the criteria it proves in `covers`.
See `.ai/skills/write-fact-checks/SKILL.md`. In short:

```json
<%#hasBackend%>
{
  "id": "CHK-001",
  "name": "Wrong password is rejected with a generic message",
  "covers": ["AC-002"],
  "type": "http",
  "target": "backend",
  "method": "POST",
  "path": "/api/v1/auth/login",
  "body": { "email": "demo@example.com", "password": "wrong-pass" },
  "expect": { "status": 401, "json": { "detail": "Invalid email or password" } }
}
<%/hasBackend%>
<%^hasBackend%>
<%#hasWeb%>
{
  "id": "CHK-001",
  "name": "The pricing page lists the Pro plan",
  "covers": ["AC-002"],
  "type": "http",
  "target": "web",
  "method": "GET",
  "path": "/pricing",
  "expect": { "status": 200, "bodyContains": ["Pro plan"] }
}
<%/hasWeb%>
<%^hasWeb%>
{
  "id": "CHK-001",
  "name": "The plans screen lists the Pro plan",
  "covers": ["AC-002"],
  "type": "cmd",
  "cwd": "<%mobileDir%>",
  "run": "flutter test test/ui/plans_page_test.dart --plain-name \"AC-002\"",
  "stdoutContains": ["All tests passed!"],
  "expectExit": 0
}
<%/hasWeb%>
<%/hasBackend%>
```

- `type` is `http` (call the running system; `target` must be one of: {{targets}}), `cmd` (run a command and check its exit code) or `file` (a file exists / contains something).
- An `http` check is a single request: it cannot reuse a value (such as a token) returned by an earlier request. For multi-step behaviour use a `cmd` check
<%#hasBackend%>
  that runs a test **you name**, for example `"run": "pytest -q tests/test_auth.py::test_me_returns_the_token_owner"` with `"cwd"` set to the backend directory.
<%/hasBackend%>
<%^hasBackend%>
<%#hasWeb%>
  that runs a test **you name**, for example `"run": "npx vitest run src/__tests__/pricing.test.tsx"` with `"cwd"` set to the web directory.
<%/hasWeb%>
<%^hasWeb%>
  that runs a test **you name**, for example `"run": "flutter test test/ui/plans_page_test.dart --plain-name \"AC-002\""` with `"cwd"` set to the mobile directory, and `"stdoutContains": ["All tests passed!"]` so that a skipped test cannot pass.
<%/hasWeb%>
<%/hasBackend%>
  The developer agent will be told to write a test with exactly that name.
- Check ids are `CHK-001`, `CHK-002`, ... Do not weaken a check to make it easy: it must fail when the behaviour is wrong.

